WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.440
SPEAKER: We've looked
at creating dashboards

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:03.180
to visualize the
health of our systems

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:05.942
and at automating them
using the API in Terraform.

00:00:05.942 --> 00:00:08.400
But today I'm here to show you
how to use the new dashboard

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.590
editor to visualize your
cloud-monitoring data

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:11.670
like never before.

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:13.140
This is the Stack Doctor.

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:16.458
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:21.940
Before we start, let's
quickly review what

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:23.107
makes for a good dashboard.

00:00:23.107 --> 00:00:25.690
Generally, you want to focus on
the key signals that represent

00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:26.890
the health of your service.

00:00:26.890 --> 00:00:28.420
These are covered in the
"Monitoring Distributed

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:30.010
Systems" chapter
of the SRE book.

00:00:30.010 --> 00:00:32.710
And we call them
the golden signals.

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:35.080
You can automate creating
a dashboard using the API

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:36.040
or Terraform.

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:38.500
But today I want to show you
how to use the new dashboard

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:40.608
editor in the Cloud Console.

00:00:40.608 --> 00:00:42.400
Right away, you can
see that the new editor

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:43.468
looks very different.

00:00:43.468 --> 00:00:45.760
There's now a list of widgets
on the right to pick from

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:48.970
and a grid for laying out your
dashboard in the main panel.

00:00:48.970 --> 00:00:51.880
I specifically want to show you
three things-- the new widgets,

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:54.550
the new layout options, and
the new chart configuration

00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:55.120
options.

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:56.620
Let's dig in.

00:00:56.620 --> 00:00:58.630
Start by adding a
widget to the dashboard.

00:00:58.630 --> 00:01:00.070
The Gauge widget
finally gives you

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:02.080
a way to show a single
number on a dashboard

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:03.610
as an indicator of health.

00:01:03.610 --> 00:01:05.200
For example, you
can use it to show

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:08.560
mean CPU limit use of all
containers in the workspace.

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:10.450
One thing I really love
about this new widget

00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:13.298
is the ability to specify the
expected range for the metric.

00:01:13.298 --> 00:01:15.340
For example, you can set
a warning threshold that

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:17.560
will turn the chart yellow,
or a danger threshold

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:19.300
that will turn it red.

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:21.550
This gauge is a good way
to represent the saturation

00:01:21.550 --> 00:01:22.150
signal.

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:24.790
If the containers, on average,
are not close to the limit,

00:01:24.790 --> 00:01:26.528
there should be plenty
of capacity left.

00:01:26.528 --> 00:01:28.070
If you care about
a specific service,

00:01:28.070 --> 00:01:30.520
you could filter for just
the resources you need.

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:33.680
A gauge is a great way to see
the current value of a metric,

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:35.570
but it doesn't show you
any historical data.

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:38.990
If you need that, you can
use the new Scorecard widget.

00:01:38.990 --> 00:01:41.500
This is a great way to
visualize a signal like latency.

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:43.618
You can also use the warning
and danger thresholds

00:01:43.618 --> 00:01:45.160
to change the color
of the chart when

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:46.662
the value exceeds a threshold.

00:01:46.662 --> 00:01:48.370
This is great to let
you know at a glance

00:01:48.370 --> 00:01:49.782
that something is wrong.

00:01:49.782 --> 00:01:51.990
If you just want to see the
current state of a metric

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:53.730
and what range
it's in, the widget

00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:55.710
can be toggled to not
show you the times series

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:56.718
chart behind it.

00:01:56.718 --> 00:01:59.010
This way you can see just
the current state of a metric

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:01.450
and whether or not it's
within a good range.

00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:03.520
The last new widget I
want to show you is Text.

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:04.553
Yes, Text.

00:02:04.553 --> 00:02:06.220
You might be thinking,
sure, you can now

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:07.750
add text boxes to dashboards.

00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:08.860
That's fine.

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:11.360
But here's the cool
thing about this.

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:13.360
The Text widget
supports Markdown,

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:15.100
which lets you
format the text, add

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:16.780
links, and generally,
make it look

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:18.550
like exactly what you want.

00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:21.160
This is a great way to add
in-context documentation

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:22.630
to your dashboard.

00:02:22.630 --> 00:02:25.250
These new widgets are great,
but what can you do with them?

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:27.970
Let's have a look at the new
way to lay out your dashboard.

00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:29.620
There are now two
options for layout--

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:32.950
Grid, the rows and columns
you're used to, and Mosaic.

00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:35.500
The Mosaic option lets you
size each chart individually

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:37.598
and place them where you
want on the dashboard.

00:02:37.598 --> 00:02:39.640
You can use this to create
a dashboard like this,

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:41.860
showing us service traffic
as measured by requests

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:44.290
across the top, with a
column of health indicators

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:46.630
on the left, and
documentation on the right.

00:02:46.630 --> 00:02:49.315
This is a great way to
present information.

00:02:49.315 --> 00:02:50.690
The last thing I
want to show you

00:02:50.690 --> 00:02:54.462
is the new way to configure
the charts on the dashboard.

00:02:54.462 --> 00:02:55.920
When creating a
chart, you now have

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:57.900
the option of switching
between the Basic mode, which

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:00.000
should be enough for most
users most of the time,

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:01.312
and Advanced mode.

00:03:01.312 --> 00:03:03.270
For example, we can switch
to the Advanced mode

00:03:03.270 --> 00:03:05.400
for a Heatmap chart to
specify exactly how we

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:08.190
want the data to be preprocessed
and which aligner to use,

00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:09.360
if we need that.

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:11.940
This gives you total control
over how the charts interpret

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:13.970
and present data.

00:03:13.970 --> 00:03:15.190
Thanks for joining me today.

00:03:15.190 --> 00:03:16.940
We took a look at the
new dashboard editor

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:19.440
and saw how to use it to create
a dashboard using the Gauge,

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:21.110
Scorecard, and Text widgets.

00:03:21.110 --> 00:03:23.570
We also checked out the new
layout options and Advanced

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:24.893
chart configuration mode.

00:03:24.893 --> 00:03:27.060
Now you're ready to use it
to create your dashboards

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:29.660
and keep an eye on the
health of your services.

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:31.710
Thanks for watching, and
come back soon for more.

00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:32.810
This is the Stack Doctor.

00:03:32.810 --> 00:03:34.130
Stay healthy out there.

00:03:34.130 --> 00:03:37.480
[MUSIC PLAYING]

